# 12. COMPLETE ENERGY CONVERSION CALCULATOR

Create an interactive energy-flow dashboard.

The user must be able to change every major efficiency input and instantly see the calculated results.

Use these primary variables:

- E_input = external electrical energy supplied to electrolysis.
- η_el = electrolyzer efficiency.
- η_condition = hydrogen processing/storage/delivery energy-retention factor.
- η_fc = fuel-cell efficiency.
- η_pe = power-electronics efficiency.
- η_motor = electric-motor efficiency.
- η_drive = mechanical drivetrain efficiency.

Calculate:

E_hydrogen = E_input × η_el

E_stored = E_hydrogen × η_condition

E_fuelcell = E_stored × η_fc

E_powerElectronics = E_fuelcell × η_pe

E_motor = E_powerElectronics × η_motor

E_wheels = E_motor × η_drive

Overall electricity-to-wheels efficiency:

η_overall = E_wheels / E_input × 100%

All efficiencies must be represented as fractions internally and displayed as percentages.

Show individual energy losses at each stage.

Ensure energy is conserved in the simplified accounting model and avoid double-counting.

Distinguish:
- Electricity-to-hydrogen efficiency.
- Hydrogen-to-electricity efficiency.
- Tank-to-wheels efficiency.
- Electricity-to-wheels efficiency.

Make it clear that these are different system boundaries.

Clearly label any omitted losses, including auxiliary loads or thermal-management consumption.

# 13. VEHICLE PERFORMANCE SIMULATOR

Create a simplified physics-based driving simulation.

User-adjustable parameters:
- Vehicle mass.
- Speed.
- Frontal area.
- Drag coefficient.
- Rolling resistance coefficient.
